\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs22\lang9 To what extent was the achievement of Indian independence in 1947 due to the activities of Indian nationalists after 1918?\par
\par
The actions of Indian nationalists played a significant role in India's struggle for independence from British rule between 1918 and 1947. The Indian National Congress\rquote  claim that it spoke for the Indian people was increasingly credible and it forced the British into making significant concessions so that, during the inter-war period, the idea of swaraj, at some point in the future, became increasingly plausible. However, the actions of nationalists were not the only factor causing independence: political and economic changes within Britain itself helped determine this outcome. Yet, it was the impact of world war that, not only encouraged nationalism, but also determined that independence would come when it did in 1947.\par
\par
The period between 1919 and 1947 saw several significant actions by Indian nationalists that contributed to the eventual achievement of independence. After 1918, the impact of Mohandas Gandhi, coupled with the response to the Amritsar massacre in 1919, galvanised opinion within India so that the INC became an increasingly mass movement; no longer a middle-class party, for example, by 1938 the INC had 1.5 million members in the United Provinces, over 90% of whom were from rural areas. This meant the nationalist movement could not be ignored by the Raj and its demands for increasing participation in government had to be taken seriously by successive British governments.\par
\par
Moreover, the nationalists\rquote  tactics made them formidable opponents. Gandhi\rquote s adoption of non\'02violent satyagraha (soul force) was enormously effective, drawing international attention to the demands of the INC. Gandhi\rquote s non-cooperation campaign in 1920, his salt march in 1930, and the civil disobedience movement of the early 1930s undermined the stability and authority of the Raj, and left the British with few options; recognising after Amritsar that excessive force would be counter-productive, the British responded with concessions in which the direction of travel was clear. The Simon Commission and the Round Table Conferences of 1930-31 discussed versions of self-government and dominion status. Moreover, the 1935 Government of India Act introduced further democratisation and gave Indians full administrative responsibility at a provincial level. The momentum towards self-government or independence was clearly building throughout this period.\par
\par
During the Second World War, the INC\rquote s rejection of the Cripps offer and the 1942 Quit India movement saw mass protests and civil disobedience. While these were met with repression by the British authorities, it was clear that further concessions had to be granted once the war was over. After 1945 it was impossible to reconstitute the Raj on its pre-war basis: nationalist leaders held the political initiative and independence came rapidly by 1947.\par
\par
Yet, it is important not to overstate the impact of nationalist activities in this period. The INC was never as monolithic as it might appear in retrospect: significant disagreements between the secular, modernising Nehru and Gandhi\rquote s spiritual vision of an agrarian idyll meant that it was ideologically incoherent. In addition, the growing bitter struggle with Jinnah\rquote s Muslim League meant that the British could exploit divisions within Indian society. Moreover, the apparent concessions in the 1935 Government of India Act were designed to ensnare the nationalists in the complexities of provincial politics whilst protecting the essentials of British power. This can best be seen with viceroy Linlithgow\rquote s decision to declare war in 1939 without consulting a single Indian. The limited support for Subhas Chandra Bose\rquote s Indian National Army in the Second World War (compared to the 2 million volunteer army for the Raj) also demonstrates that the appeal of nationalism had its limits. As late as 1939, the British Prime Minister, Neville Chamberlain, predicted that India would not become independent before the 1980s, indicating that, for decision-makers at the time, the power of the nationalist movement was not seen to be overwhelming.\par
\par
Indeed, apart from the actions of nationalists, several other factors contributed to India's independence. Political change meant that Britain\rquote s attitude towards India was shifting in this period. Britain became a full democracy after 1918 and, priding itself on its liberal values, public opinion became increasingly uncomfortable with the coercion that the Raj had traditionally used (as shown in the response to the Amritsar massacre). There was, instead, a broad, cross-party consensus in the inter-war period that India would advance towards some form of self\'02government. The election of Clement Attlee\rquote s Labour government in 1945 was a significant moment; the party was determined to see India become independent and appointed Mountbatten as the final viceroy in 1947 with the express instructions to expedite the process.\par
\par
Attlee\rquote s decision was encouraged by the changing economic relationship between Britain and India. The Raj having been an economic boon for so long, Britain finished the Second World War with over \'a31 billion in debt to India. In addition, after 1945 Britain\rquote s war ravaged industry was no longer exporting much and Britain found itself with a trade deficit with India. The costs of imperial rule were also mounting as inter-communal violence spread in the two years after the war. Britain had expensive domestic priorities with the establishment of the welfare state, as well as growing costs in garrisoning Germany, defending western Europe against communist expansion, and dealing with explosions of violence elsewhere such as Palestine. Maintaining a grip in India, therefore, was simply unaffordable: Britain faced considerable financial costs in keeping hold of India and there were considerable benefits in letting it go.\par
\par
Yet, it was the impact of global war that accelerated India towards independence and determined the timing of when it happened. The Versailles peace settlement following the First World War had established \lquote national self-determination\rquote  as an ideal in international affairs and, post-1918, the world was clearly entering a more democratic age that chimed with nationalist ambitions. Yet, it was the Second World War that determined the timing of Indian independence. The fall of Singapore in February 1942 was a humiliating defeat for the British Empire. The defeat shattered the myth of British invincibility in Asia and had a significant impact on the morale of the British forces. Although India itself was defended, Britain\rquote s power in Asia was clearly waning and the timing of the Quit India movement took note of this.\par
\par
The war also contributed to the emergence of a global anti-colonial sentiment. The atrocities committed by the Axis powers, such as Nazi Germany and Imperial Japan, had exposed the hypocrisy of the colonial powers, who claimed to be fighting for freedom and democracy. The Atlantic Charter, agreed by Churchill and Roosevelt in 1941, indicated that self-government and democracy for all were to be the outcomes of an allied victory. In addition, Britain\rquote s growing financial dependence on the anti-imperial United States, (with Lend-Lease during the war, and an enormous loan in 1946) meant that, indirectly, Britain faced pressure to decolonise. More immediately, the damage done to Britain\rquote s economy by the war meant that re-establishing the strength of the Raj after 1945 was never going to be a practical possibility.\par
\par
Thus, Indian nationalists played a crucial role in securing India's independence from British colonial rule by 1947.Under the charismatic leadership of Gandhi and others from 1918 onwards, they challenged British rule and pressed for greater self-determination for India. They were able to mobilise a diverse range of Indians from across the country and galvanise public support for their cause, forcing Britain into making significant concessions and setting India on a road to self-government. Yet, their ultimate success in 1947 was helped enormously by economic and political shifts within Britain itself. It was, though, the impact of world war in this period, especially the Second World War, which determined that the nationalists would achieve their aims much sooner than would have otherwise been the case\par
